Today is October 2026. I'm checking novelty before building a method. Context (my measurements, vLLM 0.30 on AMD MI250 / ROCm, TP=2, temperature 0, batch size 1): plain autoregressive decoding is bit-reproducible run to run on 3 of 4 models, but every "lossless" speculative decoding method (MTP, DFlash, n-gram, suffix) produces outputs that diverge from plain decoding on 37-65% of prompts, at near-tied tokens, because the verification pass processes N tokens per forward (M=N rows in every GEMM, multi-token attention queries) and uses different kernels / reduction orders than the 1-token decode pass. vLLM's batch-invariant mode removes all divergence but costs ~2.5x on plain decoding (30.4 -> 12.0 tok/s) and MTP falls 58.0 -> 33.5 tok/s. Idea: instead of full batch invariance, make kernels invariant only to the number of tokens verified per sequence for small M (say M <= 16-64, the memory-bound regime) - one fixed split-K/reduction order for skinny GEMMs and one fixed KV-split for attention regardless of query count - so speculative decoding becomes bit-exact w.r.t. plain decoding at near-zero cost, and possibly the same skinny-GEMM family also removes the ~1.6x "verify tax" MI250 pays for 2-16 token GEMMs.

Search arXiv, OpenReview, GitHub (vLLM batch invariance / VLLM_BATCH_INVARIANT, SGLang deterministic inference, thinking-machines-lab batch_invariant_ops, AMD AITER / ROCm, flashinfer, TensorRT-LLM), and blogs thoroughly (2024 - Oct 2026) for:
1. work that measures or discusses that speculative decoding is not bitwise lossless in practice (floating-point non-associativity between verify and decode paths), and its consequences (e.g. RL rollouts / on-policy training-inference consistency, reproducibility, evaluation);
2. methods that make speculative decoding deterministic or bit-exact relative to non-speculative decoding (e.g. "deterministic speculative decoding", "verify-invariant", "token-count invariant kernels", "M-invariant GEMM", "speculative decoding batch invariance", SGLang deterministic mode with spec decoding, vLLM batch-invariant + spec decode support, TBIK / tree-based invariant kernels, "LayerCast", FP32 accumulation approaches), and their measured costs;
3. cheaper-than-full batch invariance approaches (partial invariance, invariance only for small batches, fixed split-K, deterministic attention with fixed KV splits), especially on AMD GPUs;
4. skinny GEMM kernels for M in 2..16 on AMD MI200/MI300 (wvSplitK, LLMM1, AITER skinny gemm, hipBLASLt tuning) and whether any claim M-invariance.

For each relevant item: title, arXiv id + month or PR/issue number + date, venue if any, what exactly it does, measured cost/benefit, and how close it is to the idea. End with a 3-sentence verdict on what remains novel. Be specific and concise; do not write files.

